      passage:
        "Article 107 of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union declares support granted by a Member State, or through state resources, incompatible with the internal market when it distorts or threatens to distort competition by favouring certain undertakings, in so far as it affects trade between Member States. The prohibition is not absolute. The Treaty itself lists categories that are or may be compatible, such as aid to promote the development of areas where the standard of living is abnormally low or to remedy a serious disturbance in the economy of a Member State.\n\nAs a rule, governments must notify planned aid to the European Commission and may not put it into effect until the Commission has approved it. Many measures, however, escape this obligation. The General Block Exemption Regulation allows certain categories, for example some aid for research, training or environmental protection, to be granted without prior notification provided that its conditions are met, and small amounts fall outside the rules altogether: since 2024 the de minimis ceiling has been €300,000 per undertaking over three years.\n\nAid that should have been notified but was paid without approval is unlawful. If the Commission then finds it incompatible, it orders the Member State to recover it from the beneficiary, with interest. The aim is to restore the situation that existed before the aid was granted, not to punish the company, but recovery can still threaten the survival of a firm that has already spent the money.",
