      difficulty: 2,
      keyType: "inference",
      passage:
        "Under the EU Merger Regulation, concentrations with an 'EU dimension' must be notified to the European Commission and may not be completed until they have been cleared. Whether a deal has an EU dimension depends on turnover: in the main test, the companies concerned must have a combined worldwide turnover above €5 billion, and at least two of them must each have turnover above €250 million in the EU. Even then, the deal is left to national authorities if each company achieves more than two-thirds of its EU turnover in one and the same Member State. This 'one-stop shop' spares merging firms from filing in several countries at once.\n\nThe Commission assesses whether a deal would significantly impede effective competition, in particular by creating or strengthening a dominant position. Most notified mergers are cleared in a first phase of 25 working days, many of them under a simplified procedure. Where serious doubts remain, the Commission opens an in-depth second phase, which normally lasts 90 working days and can be extended.\n\nOutright prohibitions are rare. More often, the parties offer remedies, such as selling a business or licensing technology, to address the Commission's concerns, and the deal is approved subject to those commitments. Critics argue that the rules can prevent the creation of European champions able to compete globally, while supporters reply that consumers benefit from competition at home.",
