        "Article 101 of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union prohibits agreements between companies that restrict competition, and cartels, in which competitors secretly fix prices, share markets or limit output, are among the most serious forms. When the European Commission finds an infringement, it can impose a fine of up to 10% of a company's total worldwide turnover in the preceding business year. Within that ceiling, the amount depends on the value of the sales concerned, the gravity of the infringement and its duration, and it can be increased for repeat offenders.\n\nBecause cartels are secret, the Commission relies heavily on its leniency programme. The first company to report a cartel and provide evidence that enables the Commission to carry out an inspection or find an infringement may receive full immunity from fines. Companies that come forward later can obtain reductions if they supply evidence of significant added value: 30 to 50% for the first of them, 20 to 30% for the next and up to 20% for any others. Separately, firms that acknowledge their participation can settle the case and receive a further 10% reduction.\n\nLeniency does not protect a company from everything. Victims of a cartel can sue its members for damages before national courts, although a firm that received immunity is generally liable only towards its own direct and indirect purchasers or providers.",
