        "The Digital Markets Act, which became applicable in May 2023, targets a small number of large online platforms that act as 'gatekeepers' between businesses and consumers. A company is presumed to be a gatekeeper if it provides a core platform service, such as an online search engine, an app store or a messaging service, in at least three Member States and meets thresholds based on its size and reach: an annual EU turnover of at least €7.5 billion in each of the last three years or a market value of at least €75 billion, together with at least 45 million monthly active end users and 10,000 yearly active business users in the EU. Firms meeting the thresholds must notify the Commission, which then designates them; a company may put forward arguments to rebut the presumption, but the Commission decides.\n\nUnlike traditional competition law, which intervenes after harm has been shown, the Act lays down obligations in advance. Gatekeepers may not, for instance, rank their own products more favourably than those of rivals, and they must allow users to uninstall pre-installed apps.\n\nBreaches can lead to fines of up to 10% of worldwide turnover, rising to 20% for repeated infringements. In September 2023 the Commission designated six gatekeepers, and the online travel platform Booking was added in 2024.",
