        "Value added tax (VAT) is charged at each stage of production and distribution, but businesses can deduct the VAT they pay on their purchases from the VAT they charge on their sales. As a result, the tax is in principle borne by the final consumer, while businesses act as collectors for the state. The EU's VAT Directive sets common rules, yet Member States still choose their own rates within limits: the standard rate must be at least 15%, and reduced rates, which may apply only to goods and services listed in the Directive, may not normally fall below 5%. A reform adopted in 2022 gave governments more flexibility, including the possibility of a zero rate for a limited number of essential items.\n\nFor online sales to consumers in other Member States, VAT is in general due in the country where the goods are delivered. Since July 2021, sellers have been able to declare and pay this VAT through a single online portal, the One-Stop Shop, in their own country instead of registering in each Member State of their customers. Small sellers whose cross-border sales to consumers stay below €10,000 a year may instead apply the VAT of their home country.\n\nDespite these rules, the gap between the VAT that should be collected and what is actually received remains large, partly because of fraud schemes that exploit the exemption for goods moving between Member States.",
