        "The Services Directive, adopted in 2006, was designed to make it easier for businesses to set up in another Member State or to provide services there temporarily without settling. Services account for most of the EU's economic activity, yet cross-border trade in services has long lagged behind trade in goods, partly because of national rules on authorisations, legal form and professional qualifications.\n\nThe Directive requires Member States to simplify their procedures and to provide 'points of single contact', through which providers can complete the necessary formalities online. When a provider from another Member State offers services temporarily, any national requirement imposed on it must be non-discriminatory, justified by reasons of public policy, public security, public health or protection of the environment, and proportionate. Certain requirements, such as conditions based on nationality, are banned outright.\n\nThe Directive does not cover every service. Financial services, healthcare, transport, audiovisual services, gambling and services provided by temporary work agencies, among others, are excluded or governed by their own rules. The original proposal would have subjected providers working across borders mainly to the rules of their home country. It met strong opposition, notably over fears of 'social dumping', and the final text dropped that principle.",
