        "A posted worker is an employee sent by an employer to carry out a service in another Member State on a temporary basis. Posting is distinct from free movement of workers, where a person moves to another country to take up a job there. The Posting of Workers Directive, first adopted in 1996, guarantees posted workers a core set of the host country's terms and conditions, including maximum work periods, minimum paid annual leave and health and safety rules.\n\nA revision that has applied since July 2020 strengthened the principle of equal pay for equal work at the same place. Posted workers are now entitled to the host country's remuneration as set by law or by universally applicable collective agreements, not merely its minimum wage, and reimbursements for travel, board and lodging cannot be counted towards that pay. When a posting lasts more than 12 months, almost all of the host country's labour law applies, with exceptions such as the rules on concluding and terminating contracts and supplementary occupational pension schemes; the period can be extended to 18 months if the employer submits a motivated notification.\n\nSocial security follows separate rules. Under the EU coordination regulations, a posted worker generally remains covered by the system of the sending country for up to 24 months, which is shown by a certificate known as the A1 form.",
