        "In the late 1970s a German retail group wanted to import Cassis de Dijon, a French blackcurrant liqueur, but the German authorities refused because its alcohol content, between 15% and 20%, was below the minimum of 25% that German law required for fruit liqueurs. In 1979 the Court of Justice ruled that the refusal was incompatible with the free movement of goods. A product lawfully produced and marketed in one Member State should in principle be admitted to the markets of the others, even if it does not meet their technical rules exactly.\n\nThe Court accepted that national rules may still restrict trade where they are necessary to satisfy 'mandatory requirements', such as the protection of public health, the fairness of commercial transactions or consumer protection. It found, however, that the German rule was not necessary for these purposes: consumers could be informed of the alcohol content by a label, a less restrictive means of reaching the same goal.\n\nThe ruling established what became known as the principle of mutual recognition, and it encouraged the Commission to change its approach to harmonisation. Instead of drafting detailed specifications for each product, the EU could concentrate on essential requirements and leave the rest to mutual recognition. A regulation that has applied since 2020 aims to make the principle work better in practice, for example through a voluntary declaration that a product is lawfully marketed in another Member State.",
