        "The EU defines small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) in a Commission recommendation of 2003, and the definition determines eligibility for many EU funding programmes and for lighter treatment under some EU rules. A medium-sized enterprise has fewer than 250 employees and either an annual turnover of no more than €50 million or a balance sheet total of no more than €43 million. A small enterprise has fewer than 50 employees and a turnover or balance sheet total of no more than €10 million, and a microenterprise fewer than 10 employees and no more than €2 million.\n\nThe staff ceiling must always be respected, but a firm may choose which of the two financial ceilings to meet. This option exists because companies in trade and distribution typically have high turnover relative to their assets, and a turnover test alone would put them at a disadvantage.\n\nIndependence also matters. A company that is part of a larger group cannot simply count its own staff and figures: the data of linked enterprises, and a proportion of those of partner enterprises, are added to its own. Without this rule, a large group could split its activities into many small companies in order to qualify for support intended for genuinely small businesses. SMEs make up around 99% of all businesses in the EU.",
