        "The Recovery and Resilience Facility is the centrepiece of NextGenerationEU, the temporary instrument agreed in 2020 to help the EU recover from the COVID-19 pandemic. It makes available up to €723.8 billion in current prices, of which €338 billion is in grants and the rest in loans. To receive money, each Member State had to submit a national recovery and resilience plan setting out reforms and investments. At least 37% of each plan's total allocation had to support climate objectives and at least 20% the digital transition, and no measure could do significant harm to the environment.\n\nUnlike traditional EU funds, which largely reimburse costs that have been incurred, the Facility pays according to performance. Each plan contains milestones and targets, such as the adoption of a law or the installation of a number of charging points, and the Commission releases instalments only when it judges that the relevant ones have been satisfactorily met. If they have not, it can suspend part of a payment.\n\nThe Facility is time-limited. Milestones and targets must be completed by the end of August 2026, and payments made by the end of that year. Several governments have therefore revised their plans to drop or scale down measures that could not be finished in time. Not all of the loans on offer were requested.",
